\chapter{Lists and persistent updates}
A list is an ordered collection of values, written with square brackets and
comma-separated elements. Lists may be heterogeneous and nested. Indexes are
zero-based, integral numbers; negative, fractional, and out-of-range indexes
raise an error.

\begin{mullcode}
val items <- ["first", 2, [3,4]];
G:Assert(items[0] == "first");
G:Assert(items[2][1] == 4);
G:Assert(G:Len(items) == 3);
val List <- G:LoadLib("list") or G:DieWith("$E?");
val original <- [1,2];
val changed <- List:Set(original, 0, 9);
G:Assert(original == [1,2]);
G:Assert(changed == [9,2]);
G:Assert(original + [3] == [1,2,3]);
\end{mullcode}

\section{Immutability is structural}
Copying a list into a new binding preserves its value. Updating a list returns
a new list; it does not mutate an earlier copy. This holds even for lists
stored in \api{var} bindings. To maintain a growing collection, assign the
result of \api{List:Push} back to a mutable name.

Native callbacks receive list values through the embedding API. C++ exposes
read-only collection storage, and the D accessors return copies of the outer
container. Nested collections retain the same value semantics. Function
elements can still contain shared callable state, as explained in Chapter 41.

For bulk work, prefer \api{Map}, \api{Filter}, and \api{Fold} to repeated
whole-list rebuilding inside a loop. Their callable arguments are ordinary
MuLL functions or host-native functions with the same invocation interface.
